WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.167
The confusion matrix.

2
00:00:02.467 --> 00:00:06.600
Every metric you have learned is arithmetic on a tiny two by two grid.

3
00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:10.433
The confusion matrix is a two by two grid.

4
00:00:10.733 --> 00:00:11.835
Rows are the truth.

5
00:00:11.835 --> 00:00:13.400
Columns are the prediction.

6
00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:16.267
Four cells, and every metric comes from them.

7
00:00:17.467 --> 00:00:18.800
Here are the four cells.

8
00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:20.767
True positive: a hit.

9
00:00:21.067 --> 00:00:23.133
False positive: a false alarm.

10
00:00:23.433 --> 00:00:25.133
False negative: a miss.

11
00:00:25.433 --> 00:00:27.600
True negative: a correct rejection.

12
00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:30.633
Precision reads down the predicted yes column.

13
00:00:30.933 --> 00:00:33.333
Recall reads across the actual yes row.

14
00:00:34.533 --> 00:00:36.133
Why look at the matrix first?

15
00:00:36.433 --> 00:00:40.733
Two models can have identical F1 and fail in opposite directions.

16
00:00:41.033 --> 00:00:42.877
One drowns in false alarms.

17
00:00:42.877 --> 00:00:45.267
The other quietly misses positives.

18
00:00:45.567 --> 00:00:47.233
Only the matrix shows which.

19
00:00:48.433 --> 00:00:50.500
In scikit-learn, it is one call.

20
00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:52.749
Then we unpack the four cells.

21
00:00:52.749 --> 00:00:57.233
Note scikit-learn's order: true negatives first, true positives last.

22
00:00:57.533 --> 00:01:01.767
Now we can print hits, misses, false alarms, and correct rejections.

23
00:01:02.967 --> 00:01:05.133
Here is why teams ship it as figure one.

24
00:01:05.433 --> 00:01:08.733
In version two, the model misses only eight positives.

25
00:01:09.033 --> 00:01:13.870
Version three has about the same F1, but misses nineteen while false alarms fell.

26
00:01:13.870 --> 00:01:18.767
For a screening tool, that is the wrong direction, and the rollback took a minute.

27
00:01:19.967 --> 00:01:21.300
So build one habit.

28
00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:23.646
Never trust a single scalar alone.

29
00:01:23.646 --> 00:01:26.533
Look at the matrix first, then read the metrics.

30
00:01:27.733 --> 00:01:28.466
Let's recap.

31
00:01:28.466 --> 00:01:31.333
Rows are the truth, columns are the prediction.

32
00:01:31.633 --> 00:01:35.433
Four cells: hit, false alarm, miss, and correct rejection.

33
00:01:35.733 --> 00:01:39.267
Precision reads down a column, recall reads across a row.

34
00:01:39.567 --> 00:01:42.152
Look at the matrix before any scalar metric.

35
00:01:42.152 --> 00:01:44.267
Everything else is arithmetic on it.
